, and small groups of cruisers and battleships, to disrupt the logistics of the enemy by attacking Allied merchant ships. The Battle of the Atlantic ended with an Allied victory, but at a great cost. From 1939 until the war ended in 1945, the Allies lost 3,500 merchant ships and 175 warships while Germany lost 783 U-boats and 47 warships.","LocalizedString":"What started as an Allied blockade of Germany, and a subsequent German counter-blockade of Allied shipping routes, came to be the longest campaign of WWII, the Battle of the Atlantic. During this time the German Kriegsmarine avoided large fleet engagements and instead they used their large fleet of submarines, and small groups of cruisers and battleships, to disrupt the logistics of the enemy by attacking Allied merchant ships. The Battle of the Atlantic ended with an Allied victory, but at a great cost. From 1939 until the war ended in 1945, the Allies lost 3,500 merchant ships and 175 warships while Germany lost 783 U-boats and 47 warships."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Rare","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::Expansion2019","isReserved":true,"usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardPlayedFromHand"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'Anihilation_17'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Misc/OrdersFeb17/Anihilation_17.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Germany/t_u45.t_u45","SubPathString":""},"artist":"Rebellion","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/STRANGLEHOLD.STRANGLEHOLD","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":326.0},"historyPhotoSource":"German Federal Archives","historyPhotoIdentificationCode":"101II-MW-3491-06","historyPhotoAuthor":"Lothar-Günther","historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_stranglehold_photo","SourceString":"Karl Dönitz, Supreme Commander of the Kriegsmarine, inspects a submarine base in France, 1941.","LocalizedString":"Karl Dönitz, Supreme Commander of the Kriegsmarine, insp